===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A shedding of [[tears]]; the act of crying.
#: {{ux|en|After we broke up, I retreated to my room for a good '''cry'''.}}
# A [[shout]] or [[scream]].
#: {{ux|en|I heard a '''cry''' from afar.}}
# Words shouted or screamed.
#: ''a battle '''cry'''''
# A clamour or outcry.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1812|author=Alexander Chalmers|title=The General Biographical Dictionary|passage=His pupil, Maimonides, that he might not be under the necessity of violating the laws of friendship and gratitude, by joining the general '''cry''' against Averroes, left Corduba.}}
# {{lb|en|collectively}} A group of [[hounds]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|IV|i|passage=A '''cry''' more tunable / Was never hollaed to, nor cheered with horn.}}
#* '''1667''', Milton, ''Paradise Lost'', Book II, in Edward Hawkins, ''The Poetical Works of John Milton: With Notes of Various Authors'', Vol. I, W. Baxter, J. Parker, G. B. Whittaker (publs., 1824) pages 124 to 126, lines 648 to 659.
#*: {{quote|en|{{...}} Before the gates there sat / On either side a formidable shape; / The one seem’d woman to the waste, and fair, / But ended foul in many a scaly fold / Voluminous and vast, a serpent arm’d / With mortal sting: about her middle round / A '''cry''' of hell-hounds never ceasing bark’d / With wide Cerberean mouths full loud and rung / A hideous peal; yet, when they list,would creep, / If ought disturb'd their noise, into her womb, / and kennel there, yet there still bark’d and howl’d, / Within unseen. {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|obsolete|derogatory}} A pack or company of people.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|III|ii|passage=Would not this {{...}} get me a fellowship in a '''cry''' of players?}}
# {{lb|en|of an animal}} A typical [[sound]] made by the species in question.
#: {{ux|en|"Woof" is the '''cry''' of a dog, while "neigh" is the '''cry''' of a horse.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=86|passage=But the shrill wild '''cry''' of the heron overpowered the '''cries''' of all the other birds, whom it seemed to terrify; they were silent the moment they heard it, and a silence followed which made the interruption doubly unpleasant.}}
# A [[desperate]] or [[urgent]] request.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Common report; gossip.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|IV|i|passage=The '''cry''' goes that you shall marry her.}}

